# =============================================================================
# API variable catalog. Every variable apps/api reads is listed here.
# The live env files hold only `KEY=value` plus section headers. The
# explanations live here.
#
# Layering, each file overrides the one before it:
#   .env         committed, non-secret defaults (Compose network values)
#   .env.dev     committed, ONLY the values that differ for local development
#   .env.local   gitignored, SECRETS ONLY. Nothing else, never committed.
#
#   .env.prod    committed, standalone production set to paste into a host
#                panel (Dokploy, Render, Fly). Not layered with the others.
#
# The API reads these with Node's own --env-file-if-exists flag. No dotenv
# dependency. See package.json.
#
# The `postgres` Compose container is configured separately, inline in
# ../../docker-compose.yml. Those POSTGRES_* values are not read by the API and
# have no env file of their own.
# =============================================================================

# --- Runtime ---------------------------------------------------------------
# NODE_ENV          Secret: no.  Default: production
#                   `development` gives pretty logs. Anything else is treated
#                   as production.
# API_HOST          Secret: no.  Default: 0.0.0.0
#                   Bind address. Has to stay 0.0.0.0 inside a container, or
#                   the published port can't reach the process.
# API_PORT          Secret: no.  Default: 3000
# LOG_LEVEL         Secret: no.  Default: info
#                   Pino level: trace|debug|info|warn|error|fatal.

# --- Database --------------------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE_URL      Secret: YES once it has a real password in it.
#                   Full connection string. The API reads only this. It never
#                   builds a URL out of parts, so it never reads POSTGRES_USER
#                   or POSTGRES_DB.
#                   Example: postgres://derq:pw@postgres:5432/derq
#                   Its password has to match POSTGRES_PASSWORD on the
#                   `postgres` container. That one is set in the shell or in a
#                   root .env, never in .env.local, which Compose does not
#                   read. Two halves of one setting.
# PG_POOL_MAX       Secret: no.  Default: 10
#                   Pool size PER API INSTANCE. The ceiling is Postgres
#                   max_connections, so keep (PG_POOL_MAX x replicas) well
#                   under it, or put PgBouncer in front. See
#                   ../../docs/scalability.md.
# PG_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS
#                   Secret: no.  Default: 5000
#                   Server-side statement timeout, so one bad query can't hold
#                   a pool connection forever. Migrations and the seed raise it
#                   for their own transaction with SET LOCAL.

# --- HTTP ------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORS_ORIGIN       Secret: no.  Default: http://localhost:8080
#                   Comma-separated list of allowed browser origins.
# RATE_LIMIT_MAX    Secret: no.  Default: 300
#                   Requests per window, per client IP.
# RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW Secret: no.  Default: 1 minute
#                   Window in the plain-English form @fastify/rate-limit takes.
# AGGREGATE_CACHE_MAX_AGE
#                   Secret: no.  Default: 60
#                   Seconds sent as `Cache-Control: public, max-age=0,
#                   s-maxage=N` on the read-only aggregate routes. 0 turns
#                   caching off, which is what .env.dev does so your edits show
#                   up right away.

# --- Seeding ---------------------------------------------------------------
# SEED_DAYS         Secret: no.  Default: 90
#                   Days of hourly history the seed builds, ending at the
#                   current hour. The row count follows from it, it isn't set
#                   directly: 16 countries x 6 vehicle types x 24 h x
#                   SEED_DAYS. The default gives about 207k rows, enough that
#                   index choice and query plans actually matter.
